1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,110
Dancing is something that humans do when they want to have a good time.

2
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:10,890
It's a universal response to music, found in all cultures.

3
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:16,894
But what's only been discovered recently is that dancing not only makes us feel

4
00:00:16,894 --> 00:00:20,910
good, it's also extremely good for our health.

5
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:27,778
Dancing, like other forms of exercise, releases hormones, such as dopamine,

6
00:00:27,778 --> 00:00:30,710
which make us feel relaxed and happy.

7
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:35,580
And it also reduces feelings of stress or anxiety.

8
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:39,957
Dancing is also a sociable activity,

9
00:00:39,957 --> 00:00:43,090
which is another reason it makes us feel good.

10
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:49,538
One study compared people's enjoyment of dancing at home in front of a video

11
00:00:49,538 --> 00:00:52,390
with dancing in a group in a studio.

12
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:59,619
The people dancing in a group reported feeling happier, whereas those dancing alone did

13
00:00:59,619 --> 00:00:59,880
not.

14
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:08,044
In another experiment, university researchers at York and Sheffield took a group of students

15
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:12,879
and sent each of them into a lab where music was played for five

16
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:13,280
minutes.

17
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,787
Each had to choose from three options:

18
00:01:16,787 --> 00:01:23,321
to sit and listen quietly to the music, to cycle on an exercise bike

19
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:24,423
while they listened,

20
00:01:24,423 --> 00:01:27,830
or to get up and dance.

21
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:33,890
All were given cognitive tasks to perform before and after.

22
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:40,577
The result showed that those who chose to dance showed much more creativity when

23
00:01:40,577 --> 00:01:43,630
doing problem-solving tasks.

24
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:50,528
Doctor Lovatt at the University of Hertfordshire believes dance could be a very useful

25
00:01:50,528 --> 00:01:54,900
way to help people suffering from mental health problems.

26
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:01,238
He thinks dance should be prescribed as therapy to help people overcome issues such

27
00:02:01,238 --> 00:02:05,510
as depression.

28
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:13,900
It's well established that dance is a good way of encouraging adolescent girls to take exercise

29
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,530
but what about older people?

30
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:22,025
Studies have shown that there are enormous benefits for people in their sixties

31
00:02:22,025 --> 00:02:22,750
and beyond.

32
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:28,084
One of the great things about dance is that there are no barriers to

33
00:02:28,084 --> 00:02:29,090
participation.

34
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:35,260
Anyone can have a go, even those whose standard of fitness is quite low.

35
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:41,577
Dance can be especially beneficial for older adults who can't run

36
00:02:41,577 --> 00:02:43,984
or do more intense workouts,

37
00:02:43,984 --> 00:02:46,390
or for those who don't want to.

38
00:02:46,390 --> 00:02:54,697
One 2015 study found that even a gentle dance workout helps to promote a

39
00:02:54,697 --> 00:02:55,580
healthy heart.

40
00:02:55,580 --> 00:03:02,360
And there's plenty of evidence which suggests that dancing lowers the risk of falls,

41
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:08,417
which could result in a broken hip, for example, by helping people to improve

42
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:09,280
their balance.

43
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:15,430
There are some less obvious benefits of dance for older people too.

44
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:22,674
One thing I hadn't realised before researching this topic was that dance isn't just

45
00:03:22,674 --> 00:03:24,400
a physical challenge.

46
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:30,576
It also requires a lot of concentration because you need to remember different steps

47
00:03:30,576 --> 00:03:31,340
and routines.

48
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:36,877
For older people, this kind of activity is especially important

49
00:03:36,877 --> 00:03:41,853
because it forces their brain to process things more quickly

50
00:03:41,853 --> 00:03:44,040
and to retain more information.

51
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:52,527
Current research also shows that dance promotes a general sense of well-being in older

52
00:03:52,527 --> 00:03:53,350
participants,

53
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:56,500
which can last up to a week after a class.

54
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:04,357
Participants report feeling less tired and having greater motivation to be more active

55
00:04:04,357 --> 00:04:09,649
and do daily activities such as gardening or walking to the shops

56
00:04:09,649 --> 00:04:10,270
or a park.

57
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:18,037
Ballroom or country dancing, both popular with older people, have to be done in

58
00:04:18,037 --> 00:04:19,060
groups.

59
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:26,503
They require collaboration and often involve touching a dance partner, all of which encourages

60
00:04:26,503 --> 00:04:28,750
interaction on the dance floor.

61
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:35,808
This helps to develop new relationships and can reduce older people's sense of isolation,

62
00:04:35,808 --> 00:04:40,360
which is a huge problem in many countries.

63
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,500
I also looked at the benefits of Zumba.

64
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:52,106
Fifteen million people in 180 countries now regularly take a Zumba class, an aerobic

65
00:04:52,106 --> 00:04:56,500
workout based on Latin American dance moves.

66
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:03,412
John Porcari, a professor of exercise and sport science at the University of Wisconsin,

67
00:05:03,412 --> 00:05:09,242
analysed a group of women who were Zumba regulars and found that a class

68
00:05:09,242 --> 00:05:13,950
lasting 40 minutes burns about 370 calories.

69
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:22,360
This is similar to moderately intense exercises like step aerobics or kickboxing.

70
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:28,894
A study in the American Journal of Health Behavior showed that when women with

71
00:05:28,894 --> 00:05:37,652
obesity did Zumba three times a week for 16 weeks, they lost an average of 1.2 kilos

72
00:05:37,652 --> 00:05:41,400
and lowered their percentage of body fat by 1%.

73
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:47,877
More importantly, the women enjoyed the class so much that they made it a habit

74
00:05:47,877 --> 00:05:54,413
and continued to attend classes at least once a week – very unusual for

75
00:05:54,413 --> 00:05:57,100
an aerobic exercise programme.

76
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:04,418
Dance is never going to compete with high-intensity workouts when it comes to physical

77
00:06:04,418 --> 00:06:05,381
fitness gains,

78
00:06:05,381 --> 00:06:11,416
but its popularity is likely to keep on rising because it's such a fun

79
00:06:11,416 --> 00:06:13,000
way to keep fit.
